# Browser verification cadence

Do **not** open the browser after each coding change. Feature work finishes with code + tests (or the closest non-browser substitute). Keep the session moving.

## When to run the browser

Only when the user asks to **save** (commit) and/or **push**. Treat that as end-of-day wrap-up:

1. List today’s **user-visible functional changes** (short checklist: screens, flows, languages).
2. Exercise each changed flow once with **realistic input**, not a blank screen. Example: if sentence analysis changed, open a real chat line, open 문장분석, and read the generated sections.
3. Take **1–3 screenshots** of those screens (not a gallery). Attach them in the wrap-up so they show in chat, and copy them to `tmp/verify/YYYY-MM-DD/` (gitignored) for next-day review.
4. Commit/push as requested. Do **not** start a new fix loop from what the check found.
5. In the wrap-up, put **다음 날 확인**: what was tried, screenshots, pass/fail, and concrete mismatches (e.g. 쓰임 still in Japanese). The user reviews this later and asks if they want a fix.

Skip the browser on save/push when the diff has no user-visible UI/behavior. Say so in the wrap-up instead of forcing a visit.

## Do not

- Treat the browser pass as “keep coding until it looks right.”
- Hold save/push open while repairing every issue found.
- Mix coding, tests, and a long browser pass in one unending turn.
